[{"date":"2015-10-19","post_id":1236537,"title":"The Southern Cross in a Southern Sky","permalink":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-19-the-southern-cross-in-a-southern-sky/","media_type":"image","explanation":"<strong>Explanation:</strong> Have you ever seen the Southern Cross? This famous constellation is best seen from Earth's Southern Hemisphere. Captured from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rio_de_Janeiro\" target=\"_blank\">Rio de Janeiro</a> , <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil\" target=\"_blank\">Brazil</a> , the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap040708.html\">four bright stars</a> that mark the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iuLBhxZUkmU\" target=\"_blank\">Southern Cross</a> are visible just above the horizon in the <a href=\"http://www.astrobin.com/190668/\" target=\"_blank\">featured image</a> . On the left of this constellation, also known as <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Cross\" target=\"_blank\">The Crux</a> , is the orange star Gamma Crucis . The <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap070930.html\">band of stars, dust, and gas</a> rising through the middle of the image mosaic is part our <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/milky_way.html\">Milky Way Galaxy</a> . Just to the right of the Southern Cross is the dark <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yBTXpUkSGgM\" target=\"_blank\">Coal Sack Nebula</a> , and the bright nebula at the top of the image is the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap131015.html\">Carina Nebula</a> . The <a href=\"http://www.astro.wisc.edu/~dolan/constellations/constellations/Crux.html\" target=\"_blank\">Southern Cross</a> is such a famous <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/audience/forstudents/k-4/dictionary/Constellation.html\" target=\"_blank\">constellation</a> that it is depicted on the national flags of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_Australia\" target=\"_blank\">Australia</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_New_Zealand\" target=\"_blank\">New Zealand</a> .<br /><br /><b> Follow APOD on: </b> <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/AstronomyPictureOfTheDay\" target=\"_blank\">Facebook</a> , <a href=\"https://plus.google.com/u/1/+AstronomyPictureOfTheDay\" target=\"_blank\">Google Plus</a> , or <a href=\"http://twitter.com/apod/\" target=\"_blank\">Twitter</a> <br />","credit":"<b> Image Credit &amp; Copyright: </b> <a href=\"http://www.astrobin.com/users/kiko.fairbairn/\" target=\"_blank\">Carlos Fairbairn</a>","copyright":"<b> Image Credit &amp; Copyright: </b> <a href=\"http://www.astrobin.com/users/kiko.fairbairn/\" target=\"_blank\">Carlos Fairbairn</a>","alt":"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-19-the-southern-cross-in-a-southern-sky/","hdurl":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/SouthernCrossSky_Fairbairn_1824.jpg?w=1824&h=2714&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","basic_html":"<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2015 October 19 - The Southern Cross in a Southern Sky</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-19-the-southern-cross-in-a-southern-sky/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Have you ever seen the Southern Cross? This famous constellation is best seen from Earth&#039;s Southern Hemisphere. Captured from Rio de Janeiro , Brazil , the four bright stars that mark the Southern Cross are visible just above the horizon in the featured image . On the left of this constellation, also known as The Crux , is the orange star Gamma Crucis . The band of stars, dust, and gas rising through the middle of the image mosaic is part our Milky Way Galaxy . Just to the right of the Southern Cross is the dark Coal Sack Nebula , and the bright nebula at the top of the image is the Carina Nebula . The Southern Cross is such a famous constellation that it is depicted on the national flags of Australia and New Zealand . Follow APOD on:  Facebook , Google Plus , or Twitter\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the southern cross in a southern sky\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Southern Cross in a Southern Sky\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-19-the-southern-cross-in-a-southern-sky/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/SouthernCrossSky_Fairbairn_1824.jpg?w=1824&#038;h=2714&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/SouthernCrossSky_Fairbairn_1824.jpg?w=1824&#038;h=2714&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2015-10-19T00:00:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-07-29T13:45:35-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Southern Cross in a Southern Sky\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/SouthernCrossSky_Fairbairn_1824.jpg?w=1824&#038;h=2714&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2015 October 19<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/SouthernCrossSky_Fairbairn_1824.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/SouthernCrossSky_Fairbairn_1824.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Southern Cross in a Southern Sky </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<b> Image Credit &amp; Copyright: </b> <a href=\"http://www.astrobin.com/users/kiko.fairbairn/\" target=\"_blank\">Carlos Fairbairn</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Have you ever seen the Southern Cross? This famous constellation is best seen from Earth's Southern Hemisphere. Captured from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rio_de_Janeiro\" target=\"_blank\">Rio de Janeiro</a> , <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil\" target=\"_blank\">Brazil</a> , the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap040708.html\">four bright stars</a> that mark the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iuLBhxZUkmU\" target=\"_blank\">Southern Cross</a> are visible just above the horizon in the <a href=\"http://www.astrobin.com/190668/\" target=\"_blank\">featured image</a> . On the left of this constellation, also known as <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Cross\" target=\"_blank\">The Crux</a> , is the orange star Gamma Crucis . The <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap070930.html\">band of stars, dust, and gas</a> rising through the middle of the image mosaic is part our <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/milky_way.html\">Milky Way Galaxy</a> . Just to the right of the Southern Cross is the dark <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yBTXpUkSGgM\" target=\"_blank\">Coal Sack Nebula</a> , and the bright nebula at the top of the image is the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap131015.html\">Carina Nebula</a> . The <a href=\"http://www.astro.wisc.edu/~dolan/constellations/constellations/Crux.html\" target=\"_blank\">Southern Cross</a> is such a famous <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/audience/forstudents/k-4/dictionary/Constellation.html\" target=\"_blank\">constellation</a> that it is depicted on the national flags of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_Australia\" target=\"_blank\">Australia</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_New_Zealand\" target=\"_blank\">New Zealand</a> .<br /><br /><b> Follow APOD on: </b> <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/AstronomyPictureOfTheDay\" target=\"_blank\">Facebook</a> , <a href=\"https://plus.google.com/u/1/+AstronomyPictureOfTheDay\" target=\"_blank\">Google Plus</a> , or <a href=\"http://twitter.com/apod/\" target=\"_blank\">Twitter</a> <br />\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-20-when-black-holes-collide/\">APOD: 2015 October 20 &#8211; When Black Holes Collide</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-18-mammatus-clouds-over-saskatchewan/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-20-when-black-holes-collide/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\n<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/department/faculty/nemiroff/\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Nemiroff</a> (<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/\" target=\"_blank\">MTU</a>) &amp; <a href=\"https://inspirehep.net/authors/1073087\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Bonnell</a> (<a href=\"https://www.astro.umd.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">UMCP</a>)<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>","basic_html_url":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/151019/html"},{"date":"2015-10-18","post_id":1236531,"title":"Mammatus Clouds Over Saskatchewan","permalink":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-18-mammatus-clouds-over-saskatchewan/","media_type":"image","explanation":"<strong>Explanation:</strong> Why is this cloud so bubbly? Normally, cloud bottoms are flat . The flatness is caused by moist warm air that rises and cools and so <a href=\"http://www.weatherquestions.com/What_are_condensation_nuclei.htm\" target=\"_blank\">condenses</a> into water droplets at a specific temperature, which usually corresponds to a very specific height. As water <a href=\"http://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/Library/GlobalClouds/\" target=\"_blank\">droplets grow</a> , an opaque cloud forms. Under some conditions, however, <a href=\"http://ww2010.atmos.uiuc.edu/(Gh)/guides/mtr/cld/cldtyp/home.rxml\" target=\"_blank\">cloud</a> pockets can develop that contain large droplets of water or ice that fall into clear air as they evaporate. Such <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket\" target=\"_blank\">pockets</a> may occur in <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbulence\" target=\"_blank\">turbulent</a> air near a <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap101130.html\">thunderstorm</a> . Resulting <a href=\"http://ww2010.atmos.uiuc.edu/(Gh)/guides/mtr/cld/cldtyp/oth/mm.rxml\" target=\"_blank\">mammatus clouds</a> can appear especially <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mt0zXfEktuQ\" target=\"_blank\">dramatic</a> if sunlit from the side. These <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammatus_clouds\" target=\"_blank\">mammatus clouds</a> were photographed over <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regina,_Saskatchewan\" target=\"_blank\">Regina, Saskatchewan</a> , <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada\" target=\"_blank\">Canada</a> during the summer of 2012.","credit":"<b> Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/deed.en\" target=\"_blank\">Licence</a>: </b> Craig Lindsay, <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mammatus_clouds_regina_sk_june_2012.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Wikipedia</a>","copyright":"<b> Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/deed.en\" target=\"_blank\">Licence</a>: </b> Craig Lindsay, <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mammatus_clouds_regina_sk_june_2012.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Wikipedia</a>","alt":"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-18-mammatus-clouds-over-saskatchewan/","hdurl":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/mammatus_lindsay_1280.jpg?w=1280&h=956&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","basic_html":"<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2015 October 18 - Mammatus Clouds Over Saskatchewan</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-18-mammatus-clouds-over-saskatchewan/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Why is this cloud so bubbly? Normally, cloud bottoms are flat . The flatness is caused by moist warm air that rises and cools and so condenses into water droplets at a specific temperature, which usually corresponds to a very specific height. As water droplets grow , an opaque cloud forms. Under some conditions, however, cloud pockets can develop that contain large droplets of water or ice that fall into clear air as they evaporate. Such pockets may occur in turbulent air near a thunderstorm . Resulting mammatus clouds can appear especially dramatic if sunlit from the side. These mammatus clouds were photographed over Regina, Saskatchewan , Canada during the summer of 2012.\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"mammatus clouds over saskatchewan\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Mammatus Clouds Over Saskatchewan\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-18-mammatus-clouds-over-saskatchewan/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/mammatus_lindsay_1280.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=956&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/mammatus_lindsay_1280.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=956&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2015-10-18T00:00:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-07-29T13:45:35-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Mammatus Clouds Over Saskatchewan\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/mammatus_lindsay_1280.jpg?w=1280&#038;h=956&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2015 October 18<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/mammatus_lindsay_1280.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2015/october/mammatus_lindsay_1280.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Mammatus Clouds Over Saskatchewan </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<b> Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/deed.en\" target=\"_blank\">Licence</a>: </b> Craig Lindsay, <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mammatus_clouds_regina_sk_june_2012.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Wikipedia</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Why is this cloud so bubbly? Normally, cloud bottoms are flat . The flatness is caused by moist warm air that rises and cools and so <a href=\"http://www.weatherquestions.com/What_are_condensation_nuclei.htm\" target=\"_blank\">condenses</a> into water droplets at a specific temperature, which usually corresponds to a very specific height. As water <a href=\"http://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/Library/GlobalClouds/\" target=\"_blank\">droplets grow</a> , an opaque cloud forms. Under some conditions, however, <a href=\"http://ww2010.atmos.uiuc.edu/(Gh)/guides/mtr/cld/cldtyp/home.rxml\" target=\"_blank\">cloud</a> pockets can develop that contain large droplets of water or ice that fall into clear air as they evaporate. Such <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket\" target=\"_blank\">pockets</a> may occur in <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbulence\" target=\"_blank\">turbulent</a> air near a <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap101130.html\">thunderstorm</a> . Resulting <a href=\"http://ww2010.atmos.uiuc.edu/(Gh)/guides/mtr/cld/cldtyp/oth/mm.rxml\" target=\"_blank\">mammatus clouds</a> can appear especially <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mt0zXfEktuQ\" target=\"_blank\">dramatic</a> if sunlit from the side. These <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammatus_clouds\" target=\"_blank\">mammatus clouds</a> were photographed over <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regina,_Saskatchewan\" target=\"_blank\">Regina, Saskatchewan</a> , <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada\" target=\"_blank\">Canada</a> during the summer of 2012.\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-19-the-southern-cross-in-a-southern-sky/\">APOD: 2015 October 19 &#8211; The Southern Cross in a Southern Sky</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-17-bright-spiral-galaxy-m81/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-october-19-the-southern-cross-in-a-southern-sky/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\n<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/department/faculty/nemiroff/\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Nemiroff</a> (<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/\" target=\"_blank\">MTU</a>) &amp; <a href=\"https://inspirehep.net/authors/1073087\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Bonnell</a> (<a href=\"https://www.astro.umd.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">UMCP</a>)<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>","basic_html_url":"https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/151018/html"}]